            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Your 1988 house is in pretty good shape, however there are a few areas of air leakage (heat loss) that would be addressed in conjunction with completing some of the upgrade recommendations in this report:



** Around the small access door to the basement (from inside the garage) and the wall on both sides of it



** Air leakage through the frames of the older wood windows



** The bathroom exhaust vent in the master bathroom



Savings: I modeled a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 59 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.4 GJ, and qualify for $ 500 in rebates. For homeowners participating in the DHERP, a minimum 10% reduction in air leakage would qualify for a $ 500 rebate. Achieving 20% qualifies for $750, and a minimum 30% reduction qualifies for $1,000.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $550 and $1000 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.



An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior main floor walls of your house are 2x6 @ 16" (R20) and I have modeled the addition of another R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls. Some of these walls are exposed to the "outside" while others are adjoined to the garage (including the walls of your daughters' bedroom).

- Exposed Walls = 80% of total wall area

- Adjoined Walls = 20% of total wall area



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, there are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+). Most homeowners participating in this program are already committed to (or seriously considering) an exterior siding + new windows retrofit ............ so our rebate is meant to help cover most, if not all, of the cost of the additional insulation to go behind that new siding.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls by R10 (both the exposed + adjoined walls), you could potentially save $ 238 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.8 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP). If you choose to add 3" (R15), the rebate increases to $5,000.



NOTE: Without an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade, many of the other rebates in this report are not eligible. This is further outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $5,000 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.</mainWalls>
                <foundation>

The walls of your basement are constructed/insulated in 2 ways:

1. The lower concrete section has 1.5" (R7.5) of rigid foam insulation on the interior of the concrete.

2. The upper wood framed pony walls are 2x8 @ 16" (R28) and the sections exposed to the outside have the same vinyl siding as the main floor walls above.



When you are adding another R10 (eg. 2" of extruded polystyrene rigid foam board) insulation to the exterior of the main floor walls, you will also be carrying this down over the pony walls. If you also add another 2" (R10) to the interior concrete walls (ie: in addition to the existing 1.5" R7.5), than you could also increase your foundation walls by a minimum R10.



Under the new Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program that was launched on April 1, 2019, increasing basement wall insulation by R10 is eligible for $1,250.



Savings:

By increasing 100% of the basement walls by R10, you could potentially save $ 383 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 15.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 1,250 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP).



NOTE: Without an eligible exterior wall insulation upgrade, many of the other rebates in this report are not eligible. This is further outlined in the "Additional Comments" section of this report.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



The Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative offers grants of up to $1500 for adding insulation to basement walls. Insulating basement walls can reduce energy costs and improve comfort. Grants are based on the RSI/R-value of the added insulation and the percentage of wall area insulated.

The current amount of insulation does not affect the grant amounts. A minimum of 20% of the exterior basement wall area must be insulated.</foundation>
                <windows>

Most of the windows in your house are the original units from 1988. I have modeled the energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STAR?????? qualified windows. In addition, I recommend full frame replacement windows that are triple pane, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged or fixed-frame windows, with a U-Value (rate of heat loss) no higher than 0.21 BTU per hour.



Savings:

By replacing all of the above windows with ones that meet the criteria listed above, you could potentially save $ 106 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 4.4 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 2,250 in rebates (9 windows max @ $250 each) through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information, please visit our website: 



http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, grants of either $125 or $250 per window rough opening are available when an ENERGY STAR certified or ENERGY STAR certified Most Efficient window is installed to replace an existing window. Replacing old, damaged or leaky windows with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy, improve comfort and reduce noise. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HeatingSystem />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total above foundation, exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits, and excludes any new additions. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the Arctic Energy Alliance.



DHERP rebates are provided on a first-come, first-serve basis for as long as funding remains available. Receiving a pre-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation does not in itself guarantee the availability of funding. Homeowners who have demonstrated their intention to proceed with an eligible above foundation, exterior wall insulation upgrade will be:

      A. Provided with a conditional "Pre-Authorization" of DHERP funding, if funding is still available; or

      B. Placed on a DHERP funding waiting list, should funding become available



For more information and program guidelines, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program



As you research if you will indeed participate in the DHERP, please note the upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify for up to $8,000 in rebates. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP:



     - $3,000 for the main floor wall insulation

     - $2,250 for upgrading 9 windows with eligible ESTAR units

     - $1,250 for the basement wall insulation

     - $   500 for a minimum 10% air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 Bonus if you achieve a minimum 20% total energy reduction 



NOTE: If you do not proceed with an eligible main floor wall insulation upgrade, than you are not eligible for the DHERP - and none of the rebates from the list above would be available.



In addition to the estimated DHERP rebates outlined above, you can also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:

     - LED Lighting - see section further down.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT



Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.



Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.



ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION



For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.



Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:



????????? In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report

????????? If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.

????????? Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit

????????? All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional

????????? It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:

     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*

     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards

     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home

*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.

????????? All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.



ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES



You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the kitchen range hood, 2 x bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 8.3 Pascals. As this number is greater than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace, pellet stove and hot water heater all have dedicated outside combustion air supplies, so back-drafting combustion products into the home is not a concern for your house.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke &amp; carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has the following exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood

        - 2 x bathroom fans

        - The clothes dryer



If you replace any exhaust fan appliances in your house, ensure they are ENERGY STAR?????? qualified units. You may want to consider installing a new bathroom fan in the master bathroom with a timer switch (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so it will only remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



The current house modeling indicates that your house could trend towards becoming under-ventilated at certain times of the year. There is a 6" open fresh air supply pipe that is connected to the return ductwork of your furnace that is intended to bring fresh air into your house in the following ways:

    - Whenever the furnace operates it will draw in some fresh air to be heated &amp; distributed throughout the house

    - Whenever the kitchen range hood, bathroom fans or clothes dryer operate, outside fresh air will be drawn in



We talked about installing a manual damper that you can adjust towards being more open or closed based upon the indoor air quality you are experiencing. Open it up more if there is excess condensation building on the windows or if the air quality feel stale &amp; stuffy. Close it down more if there is no condensation building on the windows and there is lots of static electricity inside the house.



You also mentioned installing an electronic damper that could be interconnected to open when the furnace operates or based on indoor humidity levels.



The software I use would suggest placing a timer control on one of the bathroom exhaust fans (perhaps interconnected to an electronic damper on that fresh air pipe) to increase the air turnover in the house. I'm not sold that your house needs this, but the software models it anyway !! The model illustrates this additional ventilation would add about $96 to your annual heating bill.



Bottom Line ............ manage air flow through the house based on the indoor air quality you are experiencing.



PLEASE NOTE: We talked about your foundation moisture &amp; ventilation issues. Blowing air around in there won't significantly help the moisture/humidity levels or the air quality down there. As you have 2 x extra holes through the basement header space (left side, under the deck), you could easily install an exhaust fan into one of them. If you could then install another vent hole in the back, right-hand corner of the foundation ............. you could draw fresh air through the basement and exhaust out excess moisture &amp; stale air. Of course, you would primarily do this during the non-heating season from spring through fall.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Yellowknife



The following Dec. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61

Wood - $420.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



